verdicts.corrected_claim:
Mesa 25.2.8 splits the cost very differently from the AMD cluster's Mesa 24.2.8 build (which also differs in LLVM version and packaging). 24.2.8 builds its search structure in one thread by halving the triangle list in input order. That takes about 0.1 s, but tracing through the resulting tree is slow (1.67 s per 1024 frame). 25.2.8 instead uses Mesa's shared builder: a sort-and-cluster build run as compute jobs on the LP_NUM_THREADS pool. That takes about 0.45–0.5 s, but tracing then takes only 0.063 s. So the reading of the 24.2.8 source does not carry over.

On the local 8-core, 16-thread Ryzen with 25.2.8, the 1.21 s frame breaks down as:
- about 0.5 s of Chrono copying the scene every frame;
- about 0.2 s of Chrono flattening and uploading triangles;
- about 0.45–0.5 s of Mesa's structure build;
- 0.06 s of tracing.
The Chrono part, about 0.7 s, is also present on an RTX 5090 (0.69 s per frame). That is why the frame hardly changes with image size (1.16 / 1.18 / 1.21 s) or threads (1.38 / 1.21 / 1.21 s).

With 16 threads the build shows about 5.7–6.0 CPU-s on the worker threads. With 4 threads it seems to finish in about the same wall time, so most of that CPU time does not speed it up. The idea that a Mesa upgrade would roughly halve the AMD render time is a projection from the local Ryzen, and it has not been tested on the EPYC nodes.
